RISC OS: draw the background and opaque passes the cheap way for software OpenGL.

YSFlight leaves GL_BLEND on for the whole frame and draws the sky/ground
cylinder and the ground mesh, which cover the whole view, with GL fog. With
riscos-mesa (software rendering) that sends every pixel through the general
span path, and blending reads every destination pixel back even when alpha
is 1. On RISC OS (and in host profiling builds with -DYS_SOFTWARE_GL):
- FsGroundSky::DrawByFog and the untextured DrawGroundMesh work out the
  GL_EXP2 fog per vertex into the vertex colours (Mesa does the same per
  vertex with the fog hint at GL_DONT_CARE) and draw with fog, blending and
  the always-passing, non-writing depth test off: Mesa's fast smooth-shaded
  triangles.
- FsBeginOpaqueDrawing/FsEndOpaqueDrawing (new, graphics/common/fsopengl.h;
  no-ops in gl2, null and d3d9) switch blending off around the map and the
  opaque passes of aircraft and ground objects. The map's runway-light
  quads turn blending on for themselves.
Measured on the host with riscos-mesa's renderer (callgrind, 640x480,
particles and ground texture off, same random seed): Misawa runway 237M ->
175M instructions a frame, 10000 ft 153M -> 102M. The pictures match except
for rounding (at most a few levels) and depth-buffer ties at the horizon.

For YSCE 231e711, after 08-riscos-title-picture. See docs/PORTING-NOTES.md in riscos-ysflight.

